  {
    "episode_id": 92956,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92956.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "So I actually went to more than one school and I went to another school this morning. And you know, look, COVID's turned the world upside down for everybody, and you're sort of just bracing yourself for wherever you're going to go. People are going to be unhappy, and there's going to be something wrong. I have to tell you yesterday was one of the most positive days I've had, if not the most positive days since COVID shut all of our worlds down. That's amazing. You know, I've talked to a lot of teachers and parents and to students and they're worried about what's going to happen this fall. The kids are missing their friends and they want to be out there and doing things. Parents are worried about it. If they send their kids to school, what's going to happen? They're worried about childcare. There are a thousand things they're worried about. And teachers are worried about what's going to happen in the classroom. If they do go back, people with pre-existing conditions are worried. There's a lot of worries. But you know what I saw yesterday? I saw people that were making a bad situation and taking lemons and turning it into lemonade. When I walked into schools, they were all glad to see me. It was nice to have somebody glad to see me. But they wanted to talk about what was happening. And each of the different schools, they were in some school districts, which are luckier to be better off than others. They were giving crumbs to every student. And pre-programming them, and students were picking them up in that school system. students had had the option of whether to go to be in the classroom or learn from home and a third had decided to go in the classroom and two thirds were learning from home, but everybody was getting the chrome. But in another situation in Dearborn, my hometown, it's virtual. The other school situation we want to today is virtual as well. But the teachers are putting the materials together. They're getting the textbooks together. They're getting the lessons together. They're giving their students material. They're trying to figure out how they help connect on broadband, how they raised really good issues with me about FCC rules, which make it harder with people that they want to install internet, what to do. But everybody I met yesterday wanted to be there for the kids. And they know that they're our most important resource and that nothing's normal, nothing's simple. But how do we approach the challenges and make sure we're teaching our kids? And then when, you know, many people don't realize how hungry some of our children are in this country and the importance of the meals that they get at school. And some of the, the one school I was at makes 70,000 meals a week for these kids. And they were talking about what it was like at the beginning when we shut down. And how everybody was at home and how scared we were. We remember that you had to bomb me out of my house. But they went because they knew the kids needed to eat. And it was more important to them that they make sure that the children from their schools had food. And it was just an incredibly uplifting day.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
