  {
    "episode_id": 92956,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92956.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "You referenced the beginning of all of this, the start of the pandemic, which raised questions that we're still hearing, which is reconciling, keeping students and teachers and staff safe, while recognizing what's lost without in-person learning. At the start of this in March and April, you know, these questions existed, but it was really, it was clear that schools had to close. And now we're facing this dichotomy again. But do you think that this was inevitable or could choices have been made by, I don't know, the federal government along the way that could have put us in a better position to have kids in the best, best poised for a successful school year?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_08",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Schools were required to close during the initial phase of the pandemic in March and April.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "The need to balance student and staff safety with the loss of in-person learning has persisted since the early days of the pandemic.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "There is currently a recurring dilemma about whether to prioritize safety or return to in-person schooling.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "The federal government had sufficient influence over pandemic response decisions affecting schools.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "A more effective national policy response could have reduced the severity of school closures and their long-term impacts.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "In-person education provides significant benefits that are worth preserving despite risks.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Decisions made earlier in the pandemic set long-term trajectories that continue to affect current educational policies.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 92956,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92956.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "Well, for starters, I think that it took far too long. We should have had someone at a national level that understood community spread, and that understood states were being pitted against each other. We should have all been wearing masks. I know this sounds simplistic, but the fact of the matter is, if the president of the United States would wear a mask, he would save lives. Just the power of his influence. And people seeing him actually wearing a mask would saved lives. So yeah, I do think, I mean, I am very proud of my governor who was one of the early governors who made very difficult decisions. Gretchen's my friend, I talked to her very often during those early days of COVID, I spoke with her every day. And I actually was doing a lot of, I sort of felt like I was working for her, we were working together, but I was able to do some things that helped her. She had to make tough decisions. She got briefed about how many people could die. She felt the weight of every one of those lives on her. So she made difficult decisions. She had men with assault weapons at the Capitol after her. And you needed people who were not afraid to make tough decisions. Our numbers in Michigan are higher than they should be again. And we need leaders. We need people that aren't afraid to say, this is what we need to do. And I do think we would have been at a better place on schools if in the beginning all of us across the country had been on the same page. And we're still not on the same page. How mass became such a political issue is beyond me.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "It took far too long to address the pandemic effectively.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker’s governor, Gretchen Whitmer, made early and difficult decisions during the pandemic.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Michigan’s current case numbers are higher than they should be.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "A national-level leader who understands community spread and inter-state competition should have been established earlier.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker communicated frequently with Governor Whitmer during the early days of the pandemic.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Masks should have been worn by everyone nationwide.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Governor Whitmer faced threats from armed individuals at the state capitol.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The President of the United States wearing a mask would have saved lives due to his influence.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Seeing the President wear a mask would have encouraged widespread compliance with masking.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker assisted Governor Whitmer in ways that contributed to her decision-making.",
