  {
    "episode_id": 92956,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/2/92956.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think what people need to understand is that our economy is not doing well because of how we handle COVID. I mean, when I was talking to you about talking to Gretchen every day. The day that I flew home from Washington after we passed the first bill, as soon as I got home, I got a call from the UAW that they were going to walk out of the plants. And I texted her immediately and asked her if she knew and she didn't. I got the president of the UAW on the phone and then given my auto background, I have strong relationships with the CEOs. And there began my journey of keeping plants open, closing plants and reopening. And I did help facilitate a lot of the discussions with business and labor on some fronts. But the lack of coherence you know, strategy and has clearly impacted our economy. And I even, I look at what happened at the beginning in how states were bidding against each other on PPE, equipment, how our frontline workers didn't have the masks, the gloves, the gowns that they needed to wear, the fact that nursing homes were having to use plastic trash bags as gowns. I think that's something That should worry us. The other thing that I want to say, and I will say it very strongly, is I am very worried about how this country is being divided by fear and hatred. I think people are being pitted against each other. I'm very worried about the division, the hate speech, the name calling, the bullying, and the vitriolicness to be perfectly frank. And we can disagree agreeably. We are losing respect for each other in this country, and it deeply concerns me.",
